process-email addressed each series to the person who asked for it
and nobody else, so patches never reached lab-devel. Review there
cannot happen if the list never sees the patch, and readers of the
list cannot tell what has been posted.

Git's SubmittingPatches sends patches To the maintainer and Cc the
list. Do the same here.
---
change-id: snvpylqykytputpytzrqyosvltkyoyup

 .claude/skills/process-email/SKILL.md | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/.claude/skills/process-email/SKILL.md b/.claude/skills/process-email/SKILL.md
index 465cd84..67ef90b 100644
--- a/.claude/skills/process-email/SKILL.md
+++ b/.claude/skills/process-email/SKILL.md
@@ -167,10 +167,12 @@ come from the commit author, your address:
     rm -rf /tmp/series
     ~/lab/default.jj/libexec/mail/format-patch.sh -o /tmp/series \
         --thread=shallow --base="${base}" \
-        --to="<sender's From>" "${base}..${head}"
+        --to="<sender's From>" --cc=lab-devel@p7x.dev \
+        "${base}..${head}"
 
-`--base` adds a `base-commit:` line naming the trunk commit the series
-applies to. Always pass it.
+The series goes to whoever asked for it, with the list on Cc, as Git
+asks of its own patches. `--base` adds a `base-commit:` line naming
+the trunk commit the series applies to. Always pass it.
 
 **Cover letter.** For more than one patch add `--cover-letter` and
 fill in its subject and blurb. Put the explanation of the work there,
